/Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/references/querying.md:71:**Batching:** semicolons between queries. Statements and results preserve source order. JSON batches return an ordered top-level array; compact batches format each result normally and separate adjacent results with one blank line. A single query remains unwrapped. Syntax and unknown-operation errors reject the whole batch before execution instead of returning partial output; handler errors remain ordered in-band error results and later statements continue. Element mutations in one batch run inside one activity transaction: when any statement is refused, the refused statements keep their own errors, every statement that would otherwise have succeeded is reported as `ok:false` with code `batch_rolled_back`, and nothing is persisted. A clean batch commits every statement and records one activity event per affected element, so a batch may change an element's checklist only once; issue further checklist changes as separate commands. Local and remote mutation commands both print that complete envelope before returning a nonzero exit, so the envelope always describes exactly what was persisted.
